<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isin was an ancient city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>, its dynasty became one of the leading powers of the region, rivalling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>. Isin was later part of the Babylonian Empire, and its walls were pulled down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a> during the revolts of his reign.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They completed the walls, dug canals, won modest victories and survived in a world dominated by Elam to the east, the rival cities of <b>Isin</b> and Larsa to the south, and the Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia under Shamshi-Adad I to the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ishbi-Erra, an official sent to procure grain, established an independent power base at <b>Isin</b>, while in the east Elam and the highland state of Shimashki broke free of Ur’s control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The treasures are now divided between the British Museum, the Penn Museum and the Baghdad Museum, and the partly restored ziggurat, built by Ur-Nammu and repaired by Ishme-Dagan of <b>Isin</b>, Kurigalzu I and Nabonidus, remains the best-preserved landmark of the site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When that empire collapsed around 2004 BCE, Uruk came under the kings of <b>Isin</b> and then Larsa before Sin-kashid restored its independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The code of Ur-Nammu, founder of the Third Dynasty of Ur, around 2100 BCE, is the oldest known, three centuries older than that of Hammurabi, and the code of Lipit-Ishtar of <b>Isin</b> followed in the 20th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/language/sumerian">Sumerian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Earlier law collections in Sumerian and Akkadian survive, among them the Code of Ur-Nammu of Ur, the Code of Lipit-Ishtar of <b>Isin</b> and the Laws of Eshnunna, and like Hammurabi's they purport to have been written by rulers; statements of other rulers suggest the custom was widespread.</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
